The '''Kingdom of Galwa (1743–1822)''' was the period of the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa|western Burawi kingdom]] between the [[National Reorganization Edict of 1743]] and the [[Burawi War]], which ended with the creation of [[Kijura]] as a buffer state. The period included the reigns of [[Cali IV]] and [[Nuur VI]], the expansion of Galwa's colonial holdings in [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftia]], and the conflict with the eastern [[Kingdom of Burawa]] that brought an end to Galwa's independence.

Following the loss of [[Bindorama]] and the creation of [[Asikyira]] in 1742, Galwa reoriented its economy and diplomacy. During the reign of Cali IV, the kingdom established commercial links with the [[Zong|Kingdom of Zong]] and military cooperation with [[Breisland]]. Under Nuur VI, Galwa expanded its North Kaftian holdings and increased its competition with Burawa. These developments culminated in the [[Burawi War]] (1811–1822), which ended in a negotiated settlement and the creation of Kijura.

== Reign of Cali IV (1743–c. 1780s) ==

{{main|Cali IV of Galwa}}

[[Cali IV]] assumed direct rule following the death of Regent [[Sahra of Galwa|Sahra]] in 1740. The early years of his reign coincided with the conclusion of the [[Bindorama Civil War]] and the establishment of the [[Parliament of Lords (Galwa)]] by the [[National Reorganization Edict of 1743]].

=== Economic reorientation ===

The loss of Bindorama reduced Galwa's access to plantation goods, especially cotton and gold. Bindorama had been a major source of both commodities, and the creation of [[Asikyira]] in 1742 removed this supply from Galwa's control. By the 1780s, Bindorama's remaining gold production had declined sharply, and Galwa's colonial revenues were no longer sufficient to support the kingdom's external commitments. Burawa retained control of [[Lacashe]], which further restricted Galwa's access to cotton.

The [[Volisanian Civil War]] (1736–1801) weakened metropolitan authority across several colonial systems and created opportunities for secondary powers. Galwa developed trade links with former Volisanian colonies that had gained de facto autonomy, including the settlements that later formed the [[Hesperian League]] and the Baliscan concessions. The kingdom sought to position itself as a commercial intermediary and a provider of naval protection.

=== Galwa-Zong trade agreement ===

{{main|Galwa-Zong trade agreement}}

Galwa concluded a trade agreement with the [[Zong|Kingdom of Zong]], which possessed the colony of [[Sinchew]]. The agreement established a route for cotton shipments from Sinchew to Galwa's ports on the [[Aethiopean Ocean]]. Zong gained access to Kaftian markets, and Galwa obtained a cotton supply independent of Burawi-controlled Lacashe. The agreement supported Galwa's textile industry and export revenues.

=== Galwa-Breisland alliance ===

{{main|Galwa-Breisland alliance}}

Galwa also concluded a military alliance with [[Breisland]]. Under the alliance, Breisland received docking rights at Galwa ports and Galwa obtained military supplies, naval expertise, and diplomatic support. The arrangement strengthened Galwa's position on the Aethiopean coast and linked it to wider Illypnian politics.

=== North Kaftian colonization ===

{{main|North Kaftia}}

Galwa began a program of colonization in [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftia]], where the unified Burawi kingdom had previously maintained forts and trading posts. In the 1760s and 1770s, Galwa established ports along the North Kaftian coast. In the 1780s, settlement expanded inland, with the establishment of agricultural settlements and mines. The North Kaftian colonies provided Galwa with new sources of revenue and increased its role as a colonial power.

== Reign of Nuur VI (c. 1780s–1822) ==

{{main|Nuur VI of Galwa}}

Cali IV was succeeded by his son, [[Nuur VI]], in the 1780s. The transition was orderly, and the Parliament of Lords confirmed the succession.

=== North Kaftian expansion ===

Under Nuur VI, Galwa expanded further into [[Galwa North Kaftia]]. The kingdom granted land to settlers and to the [[Royal Galwa North Kaftian Company]], and established administrative structures in the interior. The expansion brought Galwa into direct contact and later dispute with Burawa's own North Kaftian possessions.

The relationship between the two kingdoms deteriorated as a result. Burawa regarded Galwa's expansion as encroachment on territory within the Burawi sphere, while Galwa cited the ambiguity of the 1709 settlement in asserting its right to colonise. Incidents between settlers and local authorities increased during the early 19th century.

=== State finance and regalism ===

The crown financed its colonial policy through crown companies with merchant investors. The loss of Bindorama and the reduced wealth of the kingdom after Sahra's regency encouraged the monarchy to treat expansion as a state necessity. During this period, Galwa also established a national bank and sold royal land to wealthy commoners in order to mobilise capital for colonization.

These measures accompanied a broader conflict between regalism and clericalism. Although the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] retained its headquarters at [[Weli Markos]] and remained influential in court politics, royal officials increasingly treated ecclesiastical landholdings and clerical autonomy as obstacles to state consolidation. The crown's financial reforms were partly intended to reduce dependence on ecclesiastical wealth.

=== Education ===

Education also moved increasingly under royal supervision. For much of the earlier period, schooling had been administered by religious orders such as the [[Bartay Society]]. By the early 19th century, the crown sought greater control over education. A crown university, [[University of Hargiesa]], was founded in 1806, followed in 1807 by the [[Galwa Military Technology School]].

== Burawi War (1811–1822) ==

{{main|Burawi War}}

The [[Burawi War]] was an armed conflict between the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] and the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]] from 1811 to 1822. The war arose from territorial disputes, colonial competition, and long-term rivalry between the two kingdoms.

=== Causes ===

The immediate cause of the war was a border dispute between ranchers in the contested border region. Local intervention by both kingdoms escalated the dispute into military conflict. The deeper causes included competition in North Kaftia, disputes over colonial resources, and friction generated by Galwa's alliance with Breisland. By 1811, Burawa had already lost [[Lacashe]], and the war was fought without Burawi access to that colony.

=== Course of the war ===

The war lasted eleven years. Both kingdoms mobilised their forces in the border region and in North Kaftia. Neither side achieved a decisive military advantage, and repeated operations in difficult terrain produced heavy losses without a clear outcome.

=== Creation of Kijura ===

{{main|Kijura}}

By 1822, both sides were exhausted. Negotiations mediated by the [[Cardinal of Burawa]] from neutral [[Weli Markos]] produced a settlement that separated the contested border region from both kingdoms and constituted it as an independent buffer state. The new state was named [[Kijura]] and was placed under the rule of [[Lord Cusmaan]], a Galwa noble of the Fayeha branch.

== Legacy ==

The period from 1743 to 1822 established Galwa as a regional power with colonial possessions and international alliances. The economic and diplomatic reorientation pursued by Cali IV and Nuur VI compensated for the loss of Bindorama and provided the kingdom with new sources of wealth and influence.

The Burawi War ended Galwa's expansionist phase and demonstrated the limits of the kingdom's capacity. The creation of Kijura fixed the post-split balance between the two Burawi states.

